#### Overview

## How do you keep someone booking when they arrive from a link in bio?

Booking sites are rarely opened in the visitor's default browser. They arrive from a referral link or a link in bio, inside a social app, where traditional logins and saved booking details do not follow them, and that drives people away. This analysis looks for a booking flow that holds their attention without sending them to other screens to prove they are human or to sign in.

#### Problem

## The booking site did not look like Bowtie & JP Health.

The first thing I noticed was the gap between the booking site and the official site. The booking flow ran on third-party software, so it lost the brand the moment a visitor pressed Book.

![](/images/experience/bowtie-jp-health/image-1.webp)

![](/images/experience/bowtie-jp-health/image-2.webp)

So I focused on making the experience feel human and premium again through three things:

{% points %}
{% point title="Brand" text="Bring the booking flow back in line with the official site." /%}

{% point title="Copy" text="Say less, and say it the way the brand speaks." /%}

{% point title="Progression" text="Guide people one clear step at a time." /%}
{% /points %}

![](/images/experience/bowtie-jp-health/image-3.webp)

[youtu.be/xelvpnC-hPI](https://youtu.be/xelvpnC-hPI?si=ZMAgXrIvraFuFqbK)

#### Phases 1 and 2

## First, a clearer flow with less on each screen.

With the problem named, I redesigned the flow: better guidance at each step, and no redundant information.

![](/images/experience/bowtie-jp-health/image-4.webp)

#### Phase 3

## Then the harder question: what if they come from Instagram?

A visitor arriving through social media has to type their details all over again. So I explored keeping verification inside the page, with a built-in CAPTCHA instead of a trip to another screen.

![](/images/experience/bowtie-jp-health/image-5.webp)

#### Prototype

## The flow on desktop and on a phone.

Desktop, when a login is required:

